From: Veron
Subject: [PATCH] cc_macro: blue paint -- a macro name in its own expansion is not re-expanded

C does not re-expand a macro name that appears inside its own expansion. tcc.h
relies on it thirteen times:

    #define total_bytes   TCC_STATE_VAR(total_bytes)
    #define symtab_section TCC_STATE_VAR(symtab_section)
    ...

M2-Planet expanded forever. Three lines reproduce it:

    #define WRAP(x) ((s)->x)
    #define total_bytes WRAP(total_bytes)
    int main(){ total_bytes = 1; return 0; }        ->  hangs

It already terminated on the direct `#define FOO FOO` form; the gap was the
self-reference sitting inside a function-like macro's argument list, which is
the form real code uses.

Fix: a `no_expand` flag on the token. Before splicing an expansion, paint every
token in it that names the macro being expanded; maybe_expand returns
immediately for a painted token.

CARRYING THE FLAG TOOK THREE ATTEMPTS, and that is worth recording. This file
has TWO independent field-by-field token copies -- copy_token and the loop
inside insert_tokens -- and each silently drops any field not enumerated in it.
The paint was applied correctly and discarded twice before both were found.

MEASURED: with this, tcc.c and libtcc.c go from hanging past 300s with no
output at all, to failing in 2s on a real and actionable error (a generated
header that tcc's own build produces). M2-Planet's output over its own nine
source files is byte-identical with and without this patch.

--- a/cc.h
+++ b/cc.h
@@ -148,6 +148,10 @@
 	int linenumber;
 	int array_modifier;
 	int options;
+	/* BLUE PAINT. Set on a token that names the macro currently being
+	 * expanded, so it is never expanded again. C requires this: a macro name
+	 * appearing in its own expansion is not re-expanded. */
+	int no_expand;
 };
 
 struct case_list

--- a/cc_macro.c
+++ b/cc_macro.c
@@ -127,6 +127,12 @@
 		copy->s = token->s;
 		copy->filename = token->filename;
 		copy->linenumber = token->linenumber;
+		/* AND HERE TOO. This is the SECOND field-by-field token copy in this
+		 * file, and it is the one on the path that actually matters: the paint
+		 * was being applied, carried through copy_token, and then dropped
+		 * again right here. Two copies enumerating fields independently is why
+		 * adding one field took three attempts to land. */
+		copy->no_expand = token->no_expand;
 
 		if(NULL == first)
 		{
@@ -969,6 +975,11 @@
 	to->arguments = from->arguments;
 	to->depth = from->depth;
 	to->linenumber = from->linenumber;
+	/* CARRY THE PAINT. This copy enumerates the fields it moves, so a new one
+	 * is silently dropped unless it is added here -- which is exactly what
+	 * happened: the paint was applied correctly and then lost on every copy,
+	 * so the macro expanded forever anyway. */
+	to->no_expand = from->no_expand;
 
 	return to;
 }
@@ -982,6 +993,18 @@
 		exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
 	}
 
+	/* PAINTED TOKENS ARE NOT MACRO INVOCATIONS. Without this, tcc.h's
+	 * thirteen self-referential defines expand forever:
+	 *     #define total_bytes TCC_STATE_VAR(total_bytes)
+	 * The inner name must stay an identifier. M2-Planet already terminated on
+	 * the direct `#define FOO FOO` form, but not when the self-reference sits
+	 * inside a function-like macro's argument list, which is the form real
+	 * code uses. */
+	if(token->no_expand)
+	{
+		return token->next;
+	}
+
 	struct macro_list* hold = lookup_macro(token);
 	if(NULL == token->next)
 	{
@@ -1122,6 +1145,21 @@
 		return token->next;
 	}
 
+	/* Paint every occurrence of this macro's own name in what we are about to
+	 * splice. For a function-like macro `expansion` is a fresh deep copy, so
+	 * this is per-invocation; for an object-like macro it is the stored list,
+	 * and painting it once is both correct and idempotent -- an occurrence of
+	 * the name inside its own expansion can never be expandable. */
+	struct token_list* paint = expansion;
+	while(NULL != paint)
+	{
+		if(match(hold->symbol, paint->s))
+		{
+			paint->no_expand = TRUE;
+		}
+		paint = paint->next;
+	}
+
 	struct token_list* hold2 = insert_tokens(token, expansion);
 
 	return hold2->next;
